  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY NIGHT THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 218 PM CDT Wed Jul 22 2026\n\n(Thursday Night - Saturday)\n\nAs the surface high slides more into the eastern Great Lakes/Upper \nOhio Valley, our low-level winds will turn more out of the south. By \nlate Thursday night, scattered to widespread shower activity is \nexpected to move into central Missouri ahead of an approaching \nmidlevel shortwave trough. This activity will try and move \neast/southeast during the day, but the combination of little/no \ninstability downstream and a slight weakening of the low-level jet \nsuggests any rain that continues on Friday may tend to stay on the \nlight side. Thicker cloud cover should limit daytime highs on \nFriday, though exactly how much is not certain. Some deterministic \nmodel guidance that has more widespread/heavier rain only has highs \nin some areas near 70 degrees. Given the lack of even elevated \ninstability and modest low-level jet, it is hard to envision really \nwidespread rainfall on Friday limiting temperatures that much. \nTherefore, the expectation is for most areas to top out in the mid \n70s to near 80 degrees. \n\nThe coverage of rain showers, along with some thunderstorms, should \nincrease Friday night as the low-level jet strengthens. Showers and \nthunderstorms should tend to focus across southeast Missouri, though \nthe exact location is not certain due to timing/track differences of \na midlevel shortwave trough. Given increasing instability and better \nchances for convection, we will have to keep an eye on the potential \nfor heavy rainfall. Precipitable water values are forecast to \napproach 2\" (>90th percentile of climatology), and thunderstorms \ncould train over the same areas. River/stream levels are still \nelevated from 2 weeks ago, though soils (especially the top soil) \nhave dried out a bit. At this point in time, there looks to be \nseveral critical differences compared to the event 2 weeks ago: 1) a \nweaker low-level jet, which is also more transient with its \nassociated moisture convergence, 2) lower PWAT values, and 3) much \nless instability. These factors point toward a lower end, more \ntypical summer isolated flash flood threat, if any threat develops \nin our local area at all. Probabilities for at least 1\" of rainfall \non the LREF through Saturday morning are at most 20-30% in parts of \ncentral Missouri. Those chances may be a bit low given the coarser \nresolution of the LREF, but do buttress the point that the overall \nthreat for flash flooding is low. \n\n\n(Saturday Night - Next Wednesday)\n\nModels are in good agreement that the mid/upper level ridge will \namplify and shift a bit eastward by late this weekend into early \nnext week. With 850-hPa temperatures expected to climb into the +22 \nto +26C range (~99th percentile of climatology) and seasonably high \nhumidity, we may have at least a 2-day period (Sunday-Monday) of \nheat index values near or above 105 degrees. \n\nForecast uncertainty increases Tuesday and beyond, as ensemble \nguidance suggests the mid/upper level ridge to weaken a bit and \nshift back to the south and west. This should enable at least \nchances for showers and thunderstorms to return to the region, and \npossibly, a cold frontal passage.",
